export const FR_C1_S03_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c1-memoires-d-un-parapluie": [
    `I am an umbrella, and not just any umbrella. I was born in Aurillac, world capital of the discipline, to a craftsman who assembled me rib by rib, by hand, with a chestnut-wood handle and a midnight-blue canopy that the shop windows of the boulevard Haussmann still envy. I say this without vanity. I say it so that you can measure my humiliation: for three weeks now I have been languishing on a shelf in the police lost property office, rue des Morillons, between a power drill and a set of dentures.`,
    `I was forgotten in the métro, line 6, between Pasteur and Sèvres-Lecourbe. I don't take offense. An umbrella that has never been forgotten has never truly lived. It's even our way of travelling. In the course of my long existence I have had seven owners, four of whom never bought me. An umbrella changes hands like a rumor: it passes from one cloakroom to another, from a restaurant to a taxi, and always ends up belonging to whoever needed it.`,
    `My first owner was a woman, Odette, who gave me to her husband for their china wedding anniversary, twenty years ago now. His name was Lucien. He was a man who walked fast and said little, and who always held me tilted slightly toward his wife, so that every evening he came home with his right shoulder soaked. I protected them through twenty winters. Then Odette stopped going out, and Lucien stopped opening me. I stayed folded in the hall, against the wall, for two years. I know how to recognize a house where someone is ill: you can hear the silence of the umbrellas.`,
    `Then I disappeared. Lucien had taken me to a funeral, one stormy morning, and he left me in the cloakroom of a café in Montparnasse where he had gone, I think, so as not to go home straight away. I was adopted by a law student, who lost me in a cinema; by a lawyer, who lent me to a client; by that client, who forgot me on a train to Nantes and recovered me, by a miracle of the SNCF, two weeks later. I have sheltered quarrels, kisses, a rejected marriage proposal. I know Paris from underneath, so to speak, or rather from above.`,
    `My last owner was a young woman in a hurry, who had found me on a bench and didn't love me. She shook me roughly, stuffed me wet into her bag, and it was she who abandoned me on line 6. I don't miss her. There are people who don't know how to hold an umbrella, just as there are people who don't know how to keep a promise.`,
    `This morning an old man came to the counter. He described a midnight-blue umbrella, a chestnut handle, a slightly bent rib, the third from the handle. The clerk rolled her eyes: there are two hundred of us blue umbrellas on this shelf. But when she set me on the counter, I recognized his hand even before he touched me. It was Lucien. He has aged, like me. He walks less quickly.`,
    `He explained to the clerk that he had been looking for me for years. That he had been to lost property once a month, without much hope, since his wife's funeral. That his son, who had happened to spot me on line 6, in the hands of a stranger, had advised him to come back. He signed the register. He paid the storage fee, eleven euros, without arguing.`,
    `Outside, it was raining. He opened me, slowly, with that little snap of fabric I hadn't heard for so long. Then, without thinking, he tilted me to the right, toward nobody. We walked like that to the métro, his left shoulder soaked, Odette's place nice and dry.`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-la-maison-de-honfleur": [
    `They hadn't been back together to the house in Honfleur for fifteen years. Marc arrived from Lille on Saturday morning, with a rented van and a list; Sophie arrived from Rennes at Saturday lunchtime, without a list, with a bottle of cider. They kissed on the front steps the way colleagues from the same department greet each other, then went into the house of their mother, who had died three weeks earlier, and which still smelled of wax polish and coffee.`,
    `They worked all day, hardly speaking. Marc filled boxes with a bailiff's efficiency; Sophie sat down in the middle of each room and looked at every object for a long time before putting it down. They argued, politely, over the dining-room clock, then over a tea service that neither of them wanted but that each refused to leave to the other. The old quarrels rose like water in the sand on an incoming tide: who had been the favorite, who had left too early, who hadn't called often enough.`,
    `It was Sophie who found the box, at the back of the bedroom wardrobe, under the embroidered sheets that were never used. A shoebox tied with a ribbon, and inside it, envelopes. There were fifty-one of them. All bore the same address, or rather the same absence of an address: a single first name, Thomas, and a date, March 14, from 1972 to 2023. None had been stamped. None had been posted.`,
    `They opened the first one together, sitting on the floor, like children. Their mother's handwriting was rounder in it, younger. "My little Thomas, you are one year old today. I don't know the name of the people who took you, or whether they kept your name for you. I hope so. I was only seventeen, and my father decided for me. I will write to you every year, so that you know, if one day you look for me, that I never forgot you."`,
    `Marc got up and went to the window. Sophie went on reading, one letter, then another. In them she discovered their own lives told to a stranger: Marc's birth, "your little brother, who has your chin"; Sophie's, "a girl, at last, who never sleeps"; the holidays in Étretat, the school reports, the divorce, retirement. Their mother had told this absent son everything she had never told them: her fears, her regrets, the pride they inspired in her and that she had never managed to express out loud.`,
    `"She was proud of you," said Sophie. "Listen." And she read: "Marc has become someone important in Lille. He doesn't call me often, but I know he thinks of me on Sundays." Marc didn't turn round. He said only that he did call her on Sundays, and that he was the one who never knew what to say. Then he said, in a voice she had never heard from him, that their mother had spent fifty-one years writing to a ghost, and that they had lived beside her without seeing anything.`,
    `They didn't fill a single box more that day. They drank Sophie's cider in the kitchen, taking turns to read the letters aloud, up to the last one, dated March, written in a shaky hand, which ended like this: "If you are reading this, it means I won't have had the courage. Your brother and sister know nothing. Forgive them their ignorance; it is mine."`,
    `The next day Marc put his list in his pocket. From the garden he called a service whose number he had found online and which helped children born under secrecy to trace their origins. Sophie put the box in her car, then took it out again and placed it on the passenger seat of her brother's van. "You keep it," she said. "You're the eldest." He looked at her for a long time. "No," he replied. "He is." And for the first time in fifteen years they laughed together, a tearful laugh, on the front steps of the empty house.`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-le-coach": [
    `Benoît Lagarde, forty-four, deputy head of purchasing at a big insurance company in La Défense, realized he was at the end of his tether one Tuesday in November, when he burst into tears in front of a coffee machine that had given him his change back. He hadn't cried since his grandfather's funeral. The machine's gesture, of an unexpected honesty, had overwhelmed him. That very evening, on a colleague's advice, he made an appointment with a life coach.`,
    `The coach was called Kevin; he was thirty-one, with teeth of a militant whiteness and a practice in the ninth arrondissement decorated with pebbles and framed quotations. "Benoît," he said at the very first session, "you're not living your life. You're enduring it." Benoît, who had paid a hundred and eighty euros to hear that sentence, found it strikingly profound. He also noted that he was being addressed as "tu", which his own director had never dared.`,
    `The program was radical. Up at five. Ice-cold shower, to "get out of the comfort zone". Three pages of free writing on waking, ten minutes of gratitude before breakfast, no more sugar, social media or "toxic relationships", a category in which Kevin first placed his mother-in-law, then his boss, then, after reflection, his wife, who had made the mistake of laughing when she saw him come out of the ice-cold shower.`,
    `After three months Benoît had lost eight kilos, two friends and most of his sense of humor, which Kevin called "unburdening". He now spoke a new dialect, in which one no longer said "problem" but "opportunity", no longer "failure" but "learning", and in which every objection was "a limiting belief". His wife, Isabelle, pointed out one evening that he no longer answered her questions, he "welcomed" them. He welcomed the remark.`,
    `In March, Kevin revealed the next stage to him. Benoît wasn't made for purchasing; he was made to "pass things on". He had to leave the company, take a certified training course, which Kevin himself provided for four thousand euros, and become a coach in his turn. Benoît resigned on a Friday, with a speech about authenticity that left his boss speechless. He felt free, light, aligned. He now slept only five hours a night, but they were, he had been assured, quality hours.`,
    `His first client was a forty-eight-year-old man, exhausted, who had cried in front of a printer. It was his former boss. Benoît called him "tu" from the very first minute and told him that he wasn't living his life but enduring it. The man, overwhelmed, signed up for ten sessions. Benoît recommended the ice-cold shower, gratitude and, after a few months, Kevin's certified training, on which he now earned a fifteen percent commission.`,
    `A year later, at a "caring leadership" seminar in a château in Sologne, Benoît did the sums. There were a hundred and twenty coaches in the room, all former clients of someone, all coaching someone else. There was his former boss, his former colleague, his former colleague's colleague. Kevin, on the stage, was explaining that abundance was an energy that circulates. Nobody in the room had any client left who was not a coach himself.`,
    `That evening Benoît came home and found Isabelle in the kitchen with a herbal tea. He asked her, for the first time in a long while, how she was. She replied that she was fine, that she was ahead of him, and that during those two years she had found a very effective method against stress: she didn't talk about it to anyone. Benoît looked at her for a long time. Then he welcomed her answer, and for once there was nothing else to do.`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-les-mardis-de-claire": [
    `I noticed it in February, by chance, the way you notice a crack in a ceiling you've been looking at for years. Claire, my wife, went out on Tuesdays. Not every Tuesday; one in two, roughly. She left around two and came back around five, and when I asked her where she'd been, she answered "into town", with a perfect lightness, too perfect for someone who, for twenty-six years, had told me the price of artichokes and the color of her hairdresser's hair.`,
    `I'm not jealous. I'm an engineer, I worked for thirty years for an Airbus subcontractor in Blagnac, and I believe in facts. So I began to gather facts. Parking tickets: none. Bank statements: nothing unusual, except, every fortnight, an eleven-euro purchase from a florist in the Saint-Cyprien district. I have never received flowers. I noted this detail in a notebook, with the date, the time, the amount, the way you log an anomaly on an assembly line.`,
    `In March I thought I had found it. A man called the house one Tuesday morning and asked to speak to Claire. He had a gentle voice, slightly elderly. When I asked who was calling, he hesitated, then said he would call back. Claire, when I mentioned it, went pale, then smiled and talked about a cold-caller. I said nothing. But that night I watched her sleep, and I realized that I no longer knew anything about this woman whose breathing I knew by heart.`,
    `The following Tuesday I followed her. I'm ashamed to write it, but it's the truth. I took my son's car, a little grey Clio she didn't know, and parked in the street behind the baker's van. She came out at ten past two, walked to the florist in Saint-Cyprien, came out with a small bunch of white daisies, and took the number 66 bus. I followed it like a detective in a bad film, fifteen meters behind, finding myself ridiculous and carrying on all the same.`,
    `The bus stopped outside the Terre-Cabade cemetery. She got off. I remember thinking, absurdly, that a lover who arranges to meet in a cemetery must be a man of rare poetry. I went in after her, walked between the graves, and found her in the children's row, the one I had never wanted to walk down, kneeling in front of a small grey marble plaque bearing only a first name and a single date, the same one twice.`,
    `We had called her Lucie. It was twenty-four years ago. I had never gone back. I had decided, with my engineer's logic, that you don't mourn what you never knew, and that the best thing for Claire was not to talk about it any more. We hadn't talked about it again. She, in silence, one Tuesday in two, for twenty-four years, had been bringing her daisies. The man with the gentle voice, I learned later, was the cemetery caretaker: he wanted to let us know the grave lease was about to expire.`,
    `I didn't hide. I walked up, and she didn't turn round, as if she had been waiting for me for a long time. I knelt beside her, clumsily, because of my knee. I said nothing, because everything I could have said was twenty-four years late. She took my hand and laid it on the marble, which was warm from the sun.`,
    `That same evening I burned my notebook in the sink. The following Tuesday, I was the one who went to the florist. The shop assistant looked at me with curiosity, then smiled: she knew the order. Eleven euros. White daisies. We go together now, one Tuesday in two. We have renewed the lease for thirty years.`,
  ],
  "fr-c1-le-guide-de-quebec": [
    `Maxime Gagnon, a tour guide in Old Quebec for nine years, had a simple philosophy: history is too important to be left to historians. He knew the dates, of course. But he had noticed that a French tourist doesn't buy a date. He buys an anecdote, preferably with an impossible love, a ghost or a snowstorm, and ideally all three. So Maxime, summer after summer, had enriched the city's heritage with a few legends of his own making.`,
    `Thus the narrow window of a house on the rue du Petit-Champlain had become that of a young Ursuline nun who, in 1759, had supposedly watched for three winters for the return of a soldier who had gone to fight on the Plains of Abraham. The soldier had never come back; nor had the young woman, for that matter, since she had never existed. But tourists photographed the window reverently, and some, Maxime would have sworn, had tears in their eyes.`,
    `That Thursday the group numbered twenty-two people from France, and among them a man of about sixty, tweed jacket, silk scarf, who introduced himself straight away as "something of a historian, in my spare time". At the very first stop, in front of the Château Frontenac, he corrected Maxime on the date of its opening. At the Porte Saint-Louis he took him up on the number of cannons. The other tourists began to turn to him before turning to Maxime, which, for a guide, is the beginning of the end.`,
    `Maxime didn't lose his composure. He'd seen worse. He took the group to the famous window on the rue du Petit-Champlain and launched into the story of the Ursuline, adding for the occasion a letter found in a wall in 1902. The man in the scarf listened to the end, arms folded, then declared in a loud voice that the story was inaccurate. The young woman wasn't called Marie-Ange but Geneviève, and the letter had not been found in a wall but in a Bible, as recorded in archives he had consulted himself.`,
    `There was a moment of uncertainty. Maxime looked at the man. The man looked at Maxime. And Maxime understood, with an almost painful professional joy, that he was dealing with a fellow practitioner. "You're absolutely right, sir," he said gravely. "I simplify for the general public. Geneviève, of course. In a Bible." The man nodded, satisfied. The tourists, delighted, photographed the window twice as much.`,
    `From then on the tour took an unprecedented turn. Maxime would launch a legend; the historian would correct it while embellishing it. The ghost of the Château Frontenac, according to him, played not the violin but the viola da gamba. The secret tunnel under the côte de la Montagne led not to the river but to a notary's cellar. The group didn't know which way to turn. A lady from Bordeaux filled an entire notebook. Never had Maxime seen such a happy audience.`,
    `At the end of the tour, on the Dufferin Terrace, facing the St. Lawrence, which shone like a blade, the man in the scarf came up to Maxime and slipped a fifty-dollar tip into his hand. "It was a pleasure working with you," he said simply. Maxime asked him in a low voice which university he was from. The other man burst out laughing. He wasn't a historian at all. He had been a guide in Carcassonne for thirty years, and had invented, single-handed, more knights, witches and hidden treasures than the whole of the Middle Ages put together.`,
    `"You know what we say back home?" he added, putting his sunglasses back on. "A town without legends is a car park." Maxime watched him walk away along the boards of the terrace. Then he took out his phone and noted, for the next day's tour: "Geneviève. The Bible. The viola da gamba." He was not a man to waste a good scholarly contribution.`,
  ],
};

